
## Part 2

1. Describe your memorable trip
  One place I’ve travelled to that has **really stayed with me** is Da Lat - a **highland city** in Vietnam that’s widely known as a go-to **hill retreat**.

  I went there with two close friends at a time when we were all feeling a bit **burned out**, so we were looking for somewhere that would genuinely help us **unwind**. What **hit me almost immediately** was the climate: Da Lat has this **cool, crisp air** that feels like a **refreshing break** **from the humidity** you get in most Vietnamese cities. The city is wrapped in **pine forests** and **lush, leafy slopes**, so the overall atmosphere is **calm** and almost **really charming** - some people even say it has a **faintly European feel**, and I can see why.

  Interestingly, the journey there was **half the charm**. Because the city is **perched on a plateau**, you have to tackle **winding mountain roads** with the occasional **hairpin bend**, and **at times** it can feel a bit **demanding**. That said, the views **more than make up for it**. As you climb, you get these **panoramic view** of valleys and rolling hills, and I remember we **pulled over** a couple of times just to **soak up the scenery** and take photos.

  When we finally arrived, I was genuinely surprised by how much Da Lat has **evolved**. It’s now a **well-established tourist hub**, so it’s incredibly easy to get around and find what you need. There are **souvenir stalls and cafés all over the place**, and **accommodation-wise**, it **caters to every budget** - from **no-frills hostels** to more comfortable hotels and even **boutique places**. I’d heard it used to be relatively **underdeveloped**, but these days it feels increasingly **liveable**: there are **decent** schools, shopping centres, and even theme parks, which means it’s not merely somewhere you **drop by for a weekend** - it’s a city where people could realistically **put down roots**.

  Another thing that stood out was the locals. They **came across** as **open-minded and genuinely welcoming**, and I suspect it’s because they’re so **accustomed to** visitors **coming through year-round**. Even when we looked a bit lost, people were **more than willing** to help, and it gave the whole trip a **warm, human touch**.

  So overall, I’d say Da Lat is **well worth the trip** - not only for its **natural beauty,** but for its **relaxed pace of life** and the **friendliness of the people**. It’s the kind of place that **recharges you mentally**, and I’d go back in a **heartbeat**.
